This version checks every possible starting point and extends the substring one character at a time. While extending it, the frequency array tells us the most common letter. The substring is valid if all the other letters can be changed using at most `k` replacements.

It is easy to follow, but it is slower than the sliding window method. An empty string naturally returns `0` because neither loop runs.

```java
class Solution {
    public int characterReplacement(String s, int k) {
        int answer = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < s.length(); start++) {
            int[] frequency = new int[26];
            int mostCommon = 0;

            for (int end = start; end < s.length(); end++) {
                int index = s.charAt(end) - 'A';
                frequency[index]++;
                mostCommon = Math.max(mostCommon, frequency[index]);

                int windowLength = end - start + 1;
                int replacements = windowLength - mostCommon;

                if (replacements <= k) {
                    answer = Math.max(answer, windowLength);
                }
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`, using a fixed array of 26 counts.

I used a sliding window and a 26-element array. For each window, the most common letter stays unchanged, and every other letter would need a replacement. So the window is allowed when `window length - largest count <= k`.

The largest count is kept even after the left side moves. This may describe an older window, but it still works because it only helps us avoid missing a longer answer.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int characterReplacement(string s, int k) {
        vector<int> frequency(26, 0);
        int left = 0;
        int mostCommon = 0;
        int answer = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < static_cast<int>(s.size()); right++) {
            int index = s[right] - 'A';
            frequency[index]++;
            mostCommon = max(mostCommon, frequency[index]);

            while (right - left + 1 - mostCommon > k) {
                frequency[s[left] - 'A']--;
                left++;
            }

            answer = max(answer, right - left + 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)`, since the array always has 26 entries.

Instead of finding the most common character inside every window, this code tries each uppercase letter as the character we want to keep. For one chosen letter, every different character counts as one replacement.

There are only 26 possible target letters, so this is still reasonably small. For `s = "AABABBA"` and `k = 1`, the window for target `A` can reach length 4.

```python
class Solution:
    def characterReplacement(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:
        answer = 0

        for target in range(26):
            left = 0
            replacements = 0

            for right in range(len(s)):
                if ord(s[right]) - ord('A') != target:
                    replacements += 1

                while replacements > k:
                    if ord(s[left]) - ord('A') != target:
                        replacements -= 1
                    left += 1

                answer = max(answer, right - left + 1)

        return answer
```

Time complexity: `O(26n)`, which is `O(n)` because the alphabet size is fixed.

Space complexity: `O(1)`.

An all-same string is also handled directly, since its replacement count stays zero.

A map keeps the counts for the current window. The most frequent count is updated whenever a new character enters. If the rest of the window needs more than `k` changes, characters are removed from the left until it fits again.

Quick dry run for `"ABAB"` with `k = 2`: the counts become `A:2, B:2` at the end. The window length is 4, so `4 - 2 = 2`, which is allowed. The answer becomes 4.

```javascript
class Solution {
    characterReplacement(s, k) {
        const frequency = new Map();
        let left = 0;
        let mostCommon = 0;
        let answer = 0;

        for (let right = 0; right < s.length; right++) {
            const current = s[right];
            const newCount = (frequency.get(current) || 0) + 1;
            frequency.set(current, newCount);
            mostCommon = Math.max(mostCommon, newCount);

            while (right - left + 1 - mostCommon > k) {
                const leftChar = s[left];
                frequency.set(leftChar, frequency.get(leftChar) - 1);
                left++;
            }

            answer = Math.max(answer, right - left + 1);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` for uppercase English letters, even though a map is used.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
public:
    int characterReplacement(string s, int k) {

        // Frequency array to store count of characters
        int freq[26] = {0};

        int left = 0;
        int MaxFreq = 0;
        int maxWindow = 0;

        // Now traverse the right pointer
        for (int right = 0; right < s.size(); right++) {

            // Increase the frequency of the current character
            freq[s[right] - 'A']++;

            // Update the maximum frequency
            MaxFreq = max(MaxFreq, freq[s[right] - 'A']);

            // Window size
            int windowLength = right - left + 1;

            // If replacements required are greater than k,
            // shrink the window from the left
            while (windowLength - MaxFreq > k) {

                freq[s[left] - 'A']--;
                left++;

                // Update the window size after shrinking
                windowLength = right - left + 1;
            }

            // Update the answer
            maxWindow = max(maxWindow, right - left + 1);
        }

        // Return the maximum window length
        return maxWindow;
    }
};
```

